  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A coin from Viking-age Norway was dug up in Maine. August 18th, 1957, at a Native American site on Penobscot Bay.",
        "visual_query": "maine rocky coastline pine shore",
        "visual_fallback": "rocky coast"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Guy Mellgren, an amateur digger, said he found it and kept it for seventeen years. Experts labelled it British. In 1978, London said otherwise.",
        "visual_query": "medieval silver coin closeup",
        "visual_fallback": "silver coin"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It was Norwegian silver, struck between 1065 and 1080 under King Olaf Kyrre. So how did a Norse coin cross the Atlantic?",
        "visual_query": "norwegian viking age artifacts",
        "visual_fallback": "viking relics"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The coin has a hole punched through it, worn as a pendant. The Goddard site was a trading hub, busy around 1200.",
        "visual_query": "newfoundland norse turf settlement",
        "visual_fallback": "turf huts"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The museum's best answer: Native traders carried it south from the Norse settlement in Newfoundland. Some numismatists still argue it was planted in the dirt.",
        "visual_query": "coastal archaeological excavation trench",
        "visual_fallback": "dig site"
      }
    ],
