        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "100% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 2 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Amateur archaeologist Guy Mellgren reported the find and donated the coin to the Maine State Museum in 1974; it was initially misidentified as British before experts from London recognised its Norse origin in 1978.",
        "segment_index": 1,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "81% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 2 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "The coin is a Norwegian silver penny minted between 1065 and 1080, during the reign of Olaf Kyrre.",
        "segment_index": 2,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "100% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 2 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "The coin carries a perforation, probably so it could be worn as a pendant, and the Goddard site was an extensive trade hub occupied around 1180 to 1235 AD.",
        "segment_index": 3,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "79% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 2 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Maine State Museum's view is that the coin most likely reached Maine through Native trade networks from Norse contact further north, while numismatist Robert Hoge argued in 2006 that it should be considered a hoax and Svein Gullbekk argued in 2017 that the find is genuine.",
        "segment_index": 4,
